\ apples.8th -- Thinking 8th, Chapter 1
\ Verified with: 8th code/ch01/apples.8th   (run from the repo root)
\
\ Demonstrates hiding the construction of a data structure behind a
\ word, exactly as "APPLES" does in Thinking Forth chapter 1 -- but
\ using 8th's own var/constant machinery instead of raw memory access.

0 var, apples

20 apples !
apples @ . cr                  \ => 20

1 apples n:+!
apples @ . cr                  \ => 21

\ --- Now suppose, at the eleventh hour, we need two tallies ---------

0 var, color                   \ which color is "current"?
0 var, reds
0 var, greens

: red    reds color ! ;
: green  greens color ! ;

\ "apples" is now a WORD, not a var -- but it still answers a
\ reference you can @ and ! and n:+!, so no caller needs to change.
: apples  color @ ;

red
20 apples !
apples @ . cr                  \ => 20

green
5 apples !
apples @ . cr                  \ => 5

1 apples n:+!
apples @ . cr                  \ => 6

red
apples @ . cr                  \ => 20  (untouched by the green tally)

bye
